Source package: rust-coreutils

timeout-no-core-dump-when-reraising.patch
    Backport of https://github.com/uutils/coreutils/pull/15157, merged into uutils' main after
    0.12.0 and not in a release yet. When the command timeout runs is killed by a signal, timeout
    kills itself with the same signal, so that its own exit status shows the signal. For a signal
    like SIGSEGV or SIGABRT, timeout then dumped core too, and apport wrote a crash report for
    /usr/lib/cargo/bin/coreutils/timeout besides the one for the command. GNU timeout turns off core
    dumps for itself first, and the patch makes timeout do the same, in preserve_signal_info in
    src/uu/timeout/src/platform/unix.rs.

    The patch adds test_child_core_dump_not_repeated to tests/by-util/test_timeout.rs, which fails
    without the fix. The build runs it with the two patches below, but debian/rules ignores the
    result of the tests, so check the build log for "test_timeout::test_child_core_dump_not_repeated
    ... ok" after a version bump. From 0.12.0 on, Ubuntu's build runs none of tests/tests.rs,
    because binary_name_execfn_fallback in tests/test_util_name.rs fails under Ubuntu's
    require-utility-to-be-invoked-at-matching-path.patch and cargo stops at that test binary, see
    https://bugs.launchpad.net/ubuntu/+source/rust-coreutils/+bug/2174403. Once 26.04 moves to
    0.12.0, the line is therefore missing until that bug is fixed. Drop the patch once Ubuntu ships
    a uutils release which includes the pull request, the first after 0.12.0.

test-skip-list-stray-word.patch (debian-series)
    The --skip options which override_dh_auto_test in debian/rules passes to cargo test include a
    stray word, stdout, which cargo treats as a test name filter, so the build ran only the 27 tests
    with stdout in their names, of more than 5,500 in tests/tests.rs. Ubuntu's packaging dropped the
    word in 0.11.0-2ubuntu1, and the patch backports that. Drop it once Ubuntu's version in 26.04
    lacks the word.

test-build-panic-unwind.patch (debian-series)
    Lets the tests compile with many jobs. The release profile sets panic = "abort", but a test
    harness needs unwinding, so cargo test builds uu_stdbuf_libstdbuf twice, with panic=abort for
    the coreutils binary and with panic=unwind for the test binaries. The crate is a cdylib, whose
    file name has no hash, so both variants are linked to target/release/deps/libstdbuf.so, which
    cargo warns about as an "output filename collision". With thin LTO, which
    Tweak-release-build-profile.patch sets, both also write their intermediate objects into
    target/release/deps, named after the output, so the two links read and rewrite the same
    objects. With 16 and with 24 jobs one link failed with "file truncated", and no test ran. The
    patch sets CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_PANIC=unwind for the test step only, so that one variant
    exists, and the install step still builds the installed binaries with panic=abort. The collision
    is reported in https://github.com/uutils/coreutils/issues/16422, and uutils' main still sets
    panic = "abort" and has uu_stdbuf depend on uu_stdbuf_libstdbuf, so drop the patch only once
    the test step of a build no longer warns about the collision without it.

    With both patches, builds with 24 jobs in a container ran all of tests/tests.rs: 5,424 tests
    passed, 82 were ignored, and 3 failed, in date, head and ls, in one build also one in install.
